-- title:
--   Reference events $\mathcal B$ (Borel subsets of $[0,1]$) and open or closed reference intervals
-- statement:
--   The decision maker calibrates her beliefs against a uniform randomising device, such as a probability wheel, whose events are represented by subsets of $[0,1]$. The **reference events** form the family
--
--   $$\mathcal B=\{E\subseteq[0,1] : E \text{ Borel measurable}\},$$
--
--   the σ-field on $[0,1]$ generated by its open and closed subintervals. The same $\mathcal B$ is used in every small world.
--
--   A **reference interval** of length $\ell$ is a set $I=[a,b]$ or $I=(a,b)$ with $0\le a\le b\le 1$ and $\ell=b-a$. Degenerate intervals are included: $[a,a]=\{a\}$ and $(a,a)=\emptyset$ both have length $0$. These are the intervals of Axiom PSW2, which says that intervals compare in likelihood by their length.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Only open and closed intervals are reference intervals, as in Axiom PSW2; half-open intervals are not, and their likelihood follows from the axioms. The length is well defined, since a nonempty closed interval determines its endpoints and is never open.
-- source:
--   French, Axiomatising the Bayesian Paradigm in Parallel Small Worlds, author's accepted manuscript OPRE-2018-05-258 (WRAP 118263), §4, p.20 (PDF p.21), definition of B and Axiom PSW2

import Mathlib

namespace ParallelSmallWorlds.SEU

/-- **The reference σ-field `B` of the uniform randomising device.**
S. French, *Axiomatising the Bayesian Paradigm in Parallel Small Worlds*, author's accepted
manuscript OPRE-2018-05-258 (WRAP 118263), §4, p.20 (PDF p.21): "We let B be the σ-field of open
and closed intervals on [0,1]. To be absolutely clear: we used the same B in comparisons with
Qw, ∀w."

`RefEvents` is the family of Borel (measurable) subsets of `[0,1]`, i.e. the σ-field on `[0,1]`
generated by its open and closed subintervals. The same `RefEvents` is used in every small world.

**Formalization Note.** "The σ-field of open and closed intervals" is read as the σ-field these
intervals generate (reading R5). It is closed under the unions that Axioms PSW4 and PSW5 form. -/
def RefEvents : Set (Set ℝ) :=
  {E | MeasurableSet E ∧ E ⊆ Set.Icc 0 1}

/-- **An open or closed reference interval `I ⊆ [0,1]` of length `ℓ`** (Axiom PSW2, p.20,
PDF p.21: "Let I, J be intervals, open or closed, in B with lengths ℓI and ℓJ").

`IsRefInterval I ℓ` holds when `I = [a,b]` or `I = (a,b)` for some `0 ≤ a ≤ b ≤ 1` and
`ℓ = b − a`.

**Formalization Note.** Only open and closed intervals are included, as printed; half-open
intervals are not (adding them would strengthen Axiom PSW2). Degenerate intervals are included:
`[a,a] = {a}` and `(a,a) = ∅`, both of length `0`. The length is well defined: a nonempty closed
interval determines its endpoints, a nonempty open interval is never closed, and `∅` is only of
the form `(a,a)`. -/
def IsRefInterval (I : Set ℝ) (ℓ : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ a b : ℝ, 0 ≤ a ∧ a ≤ b ∧ b ≤ 1 ∧ (I = Set.Icc a b ∨ I = Set.Ioo a b) ∧ ℓ = b - a

end ParallelSmallWorlds.SEU


